<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Divisibility and the Division Algorithm</title>

    <page>
        <p>
            <term>Definition:</term> A nonzero integer <m>a</m> is said to
            <term>divide</term> an integer <m>b</m> if <m>b = ak</m> for some
            integer <m>k</m>.
            When <m>a</m> does divide <m>b</m>, we write <q> <m>a\mid b</m> </q>
            and when <m>a</m> does not divide <m>b</m>, we write
            <q> <m>a\nmid b</m> </q>.
        </p>

        <activity workspace=".8in" xml:id="act-02-4-SetsDivides">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Answer the following 4 questions about each pair.
                    On the next page there are some example triangles to guide
                    your discussion.
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Is there any integer that is in both sets?
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Does the left set contain all of the members of
                                the right?
                                Does the right set contain all the members on
                                the left?
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Can you say anything about the relationship
                                between the sets?
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                If you use an oval region to represent one set,
                                how would you portray the other set in that
                                pair?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>A = \{ x\in\mathbb{Z}: 3\mid x \}</m>
                        <m>B = \{ x\in\mathbb{Z} : 2\mid x\}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>A = \{ x\in\mathbb{Z}: 3\mid x \}</m>
                        <m>C = \{ x\in\mathbb{Z} : 3\mid 2x\}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>A = \{ x\in\mathbb{Z}: 3\mid x \}</m>
                        <m>D = \{ x\in\mathbb{Z} : 6\mid x\}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>A = \{ x\in\mathbb{Z}: 3\mid x \}</m>
                        <m>E = \{ 2x\in\mathbb{Z} :x \text{ is prime}\}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>A = \{ x\in\mathbb{Z}: 3\mid x \}</m>
                        <m>F = \{ x\in\mathbb{Z} : 3 \nmid x^2-1\}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>A = \{ x\in\mathbb{Z}: 3\mid x \}</m>
                        <m>G = \{ x^2-1\in\mathbb{Z} : 3 \mid x\}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    With your team, prove one of the following propositions:
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For all integers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>, if
                        <m>m\mid n</m> then <m>m\mid (7n^3 + 13n^2 - n)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For all integers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>, if
                        <m>m\mid n</m>, then <m>m^2\mid n^2</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For all integers <m>a, b</m> and <m>c</m>, and all
                        integers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>, if <m>a\mid b</m> and
                        <m>a\mid c</m> then <m>a\mid bm+cn</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>

        <p workspace="5in">
            Space to work:
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <theorem>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    (The Division Algorithm) For all integers <m>a</m> and
                    <m>m</m> with <m>m&gt;0</m>, there exist unique integers
                    <m>q</m> and <m>r</m> such that
                    <md>
                        a = mq + r
                    </md>
                    where <m>0 \le r &lt; m</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <remark>
            <p>
                Here's some comments about the Division Algorithm:
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The Division Algorithm can be proven is not so much
                            an algorithm as it is a theorem.
                            One can prove the Division Algorithm and your
                            textbook does in the Bonus Examples of Chapter 7,
                            and you may see a proof if you take MTH 350.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Though there are several different ways to write
                            <m>a=bq+r</m> there's only ONE way to write
                            <m>a=bq+r</m> with <m>0\le r &lt; b</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We call <m>q</m> the quotient and <m>r</m> the
                            remainder.
                            (Notice we are using <q>the</q> because the quotient
                            and remainder are unique!)
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Be careful with negative numbers.
                            In a calculator <m>-17/5 = -3.4</m>.
                            It would be natural to think this means the quotient
                            when dividing <m>-17</m> by <m>5</m> is <m>-3</m>.
                            However, <m>-17=5(-4)+3</m>, giving quotient
                            <m>-4</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            One way to look at the Division Algorithm is that an
                            integer is either going to be a multiple of <m>b</m>
                            or between multiples of <m>b</m>.
                            Thus, the Division Algorithm helps with cases, e.g.,
                            you can do cases based on the remainder.
                            In this way, we might reduce infinitely many
                            integers, down to <m>3</m> <q>types</q> of integers
                            - e.g., what the remainder is when divided by
                            <m>3</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </remark>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In problems where we are concerned with the issue of
                    <q>divides,</q> the Division Algorithm provides a natural
                    way to consider cases.
                    Consider <xref ref="thm-02-4-littlefermat3"/>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-02-4-littlefermat3">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>n</m> is an integer then <m>3\mid n^3-n</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <p workspace="1in">
            Test the theorem for several values of <m>n</m>.
            (Include both positive and negative, and even and odd values!)
        </p>

        <p>
            The only hypothesis is <q> <m>n</m> is an integer</q>.
            This doesn't give us a lot to go on.
            Suppose instead, we have the theorem:
        </p>

        <theorem>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>n</m> is an integer and <m>3\mid n</m>, then
                    <m>3\mid n^3-n</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <p workspace="3in">
            Do a <q>pre-proof analysis</q> of this theorem!
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            I claim that with the proof from the previous question, we are
            actually 1/3 of the way to a proof.
            What are the possibilities for the remainder when <m>n</m> is
            divided by <m>3</m>?
            How can this lead to cases?
        </p>

        <p>
            Read the proof below out loud.
            <fn>This proof is from <url href="https://gvsuoer.github.io/sundstrom-textbook/S_divalgo.html\#P\_3divides">https://gvsuoer.github.io/sundstrom-textbook/S_divalgo.html\#P\_3divides</url></fn>.
            Pause after each sentence to make sure you understand what the
            sentence means and why it is said.
            Make notes on the proof of any details that help you make sense of
            it!
            Switch who is reading at least every paragraph!
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-casesproof">
            <caption>A proof using cases.</caption>
            <image source="images/CasesProof.png" width="70%">
                <shortdescription>
                    A proof of the previous theorem.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            What questions do you have on the proof?
            How does the author set up the structure of a proof using cases?
            Can you prove the last case?
            Can you generalize?
            E.g., is it true that <m>2\mid n^2 - n</m>?
            <m>4\mid n^4-n</m>?
            <fn>This theorem is actually a corollary of Fermat's Little Theorem (Theorem 2.19 on page 71 of your textbook).</fn>
        </p>
    </page>
</handout>